(If scores are too flat, it falls back to equal weighting.) Tj
T*
T*
(### `buildRecommendations\(...\)`) Tj
T*
T*
(This compares current allocation weight against target allocation weight and assigns:) Tj
T*
T*
(- `BUY` if target weight is meaningfully above current weight) Tj
T*
(- `SELL` if target weight is meaningfully below current weight) Tj
T*
(- `HOLD` if the difference is small) Tj
T*
T*
(These actions are what appear in the frontend recommendations table.) Tj
T*
T*
(### `weightedExpectedReturn\(...\)`, `weightedExpectedRisk\(...\)`, and) Tj
T*
(`calculateSharpeRatio\(...\)`) Tj
T*
T*
(These methods produce portfolio-level summary metrics:) Tj
T*
T*
(- expected return) Tj
T*
(- expected risk) Tj
T*
(- Sharpe ratio) Tj
T*
T*
(The Sharpe ratio gives a simple measure of return relative to risk.) Tj
T*
T*
(Why It Is Called Quantum-Inspired) Tj
T*
T*
(It is called quantum-inspired because it frames the problem as an optimization task and) Tj
T*
(ranks assets under constraints, similar to how advanced optimization systems are often) Tj
T*
(presented in finance and quantum research discussions.) Tj
T*
T*
(However, this implementation is still classical. It does not:) Tj
T*
T*
(- create qubits) Tj
T*
(- execute quantum circuits) Tj
T*
(- call IBM Qiskit, D-Wave, Cirq, or PennyLane) Tj
T*
(- run on quantum hardware) Tj
T*
T*
(So the current version is best described as a quantum-inspired optimization demo.) Tj
T*
T*
(Impact of This Feature) Tj
T*
T*
(This feature adds value because it:) Tj
T*
T*
(- demonstrates advanced portfolio optimization concepts) Tj
T*
(- gives users interpretable buy/sell/hold guidance) Tj
T*
(- creates a strong technical showcase for the project) Tj
ET
endstream
