1 0 0 1 54 738 Tm
(- keeps a clean API contract that could later support a more advanced optimizer) Tj
T*
T*
(Limitations) Tj
T*
T*
(The current implementation has important limitations:) Tj
T*
T*
(- it is not true quantum computing) Tj
T*
(- it uses heuristic scoring rather than a mathematically exact global optimizer) Tj
T*
(- it depends on market history availability) Tj
T*
(- it performs per-symbol history fetching, which can increase latency and API usage) Tj
T*
(- recommendation quality depends on the quality of fallback data) Tj
T*
T*
(What Would Happen at Scale) Tj
T*
T*
(If this exact implementation were deployed at scale, the main issues would be:) Tj
T*
T*
(### 1. Higher latency) Tj
T*
T*
(The optimizer fetches historical data for each symbol. Larger portfolios and more users) Tj
T*
(would increase response time.) Tj
T*
T*
(### 2. API quota pressure) Tj
T*
T*
(Repeated history lookups across many users would put pressure on external market-data APIs.) Tj
T*
T*
(### 3. More backend load) Tj
T*
T*
(All optimization work currently runs synchronously in the request path. High traffic would) Tj
T*
(increase CPU and thread pressure.) Tj
T*
T*
(### 4. Inconsistent result quality) Tj
T*
T*
(When historical data is unavailable, the service falls back to simpler metrics. That means) Tj
T*
(recommendation quality can vary.) Tj
T*
T*
(### 5. No real quantum advantage) Tj
T*
T*
(Scaling this service would only scale a classical heuristic optimizer, not a true quantum) Tj
T*
(engine.) Tj
T*
T*
(What Would Be Needed for Production Scale) Tj
T*
T*
(To support large-scale deployment, the system would likely need:) Tj
T*
T*
(- cached and preprocessed historical market data) Tj
ET
endstream
